<figure class="r54-snapshots" aria-labelledby="r54-snapshots-title">
 <figcaption id="r54-snapshots-title"><span>Figure 1</span> Two consistent remote snapshots can describe no common instant</figcaption>
 <p>Observed in PostgreSQL 18.6 with postgres_fdw 1.2. Separate scratch updates; canonical prices are restored afterwards.</p>
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Local transaction reads A.</strong><span>A’s remote snapshot sees shop 10 P7 at 20.00.</span></li>
  <li><strong>A commits its update.</strong><span>A’s current price becomes 21.00.</span></li>
  <li><strong>B commits its later update.</strong><span>B’s current shop 20 P7 price becomes 19.00.</span></li>
  <li><strong>The local transaction reads again.</strong><span>A still returns 20.00. First access to B returns 19.00.</span></li>
 </ol>
 <div class="pair"><strong>Returned pair: A 20.00 · B 19.00</strong><span>Before B reached 19.00, A had already left 20.00.</span></div>
</figure>
